21ab0cb0","position":"1788763047947026001-8e3c4cee-942f-446f-abe7-d31403d59c58"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"close_class":"preexisting","comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"OBSERVATION\nThe installed GC script `/Users/joeoakhart/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh` acquires an atomic directory lock at lines 70-75 (`mkdir \"$LOCK\"`; `trap 'rmdir \"$LOCK\" ...' EXIT`) before scanning worktrees. Inside the sweep it runs `dirty=\"$(git -C \"$WT\" status --porcelain ... )\"` at lines 188-190 with no wall-clock bound. If that `git status` blocks, the lock remains held, and the only behavior on a later run is `SKIP: another gc run in progress`. If the process dies without running the EXIT trap (e.g. SIGKILL), the lock directory has no holder metadata and remains indistinguishable from a live holder. The idle test later uses `find \"$WT\" -type f ... -mmin` without excluding `.rebar/usage.jsonl` or `src/rebar/_build_info.py`, so rebar's own generated files can keep a worktree classified active.\n\nHYPOTHESIS\nThe outage is caused by doing an unbounded potentially-blocking filesystem/git probe inside a single-flight lock whose state is just a directory. A transient IO stall becomes a permanent service outage because the lock serializes the whole GC and has no timeout, heartbeat, PID, or stale-holder recovery. The misleading `launchctl` surface then hides the outage because a still-running PID plus exit-code column `0` looks healthy unless elapsed time/last-run stamp are checked.\n\nCONFIRMATION\nI ran a bounded scratch reproduction of the locking mechanism: one process did `mkdir lock; trap rmdir EXIT; sleep 30` under `perl -e 'alarm shift; exec @ARGV' 8 ...` to stand in for blocked `git status`; a second acquisition attempt while it slept failed and printed the equivalent of `SKIP`. I then created a dead-holder lock directory with no process and confirmed a subsequent `mkdir` still failed, proving the current lock shape cannot distinguish live vs stale. This would have been disproven if the second run could proceed independently or if stale directory locks carried enough holder state to self-recover.\n\nSUGGESTED FIX DIRECTION\nBound each per-worktree `git status` (macOS-safe `perl alarm`/available bounder) and classify timeout as `unknown -> KEEP`. Replace bare mkdir locks with PID/heartbeat metadata plus live-holder and stale-holder branches, and clean up on EXIT/TERM. Exclude known generated noise from the idle mtime scan, and surface stale last-run/overlong active-run status somewhere other than `ps`.","timestamp":1788737328114003001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Plan review PASS. RCA sanity-check: the root cause is an unbounded per-worktree git status while holding a bare mkdir lock; bounding status as unknown->keep plus stale-holder metadata recovery is a straightforward bug fix, not a design decision.","timestamp":1788744788110241001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Skipped implementation in this pass after sanity-checking the RCA against the repo: the failing script is the host-local installed ~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh and no managed source for that LaunchAgent/GC script exists in this repository. Landing a Gerrit change would require first deciding whether to vendor/manage that operator script in the repo versus patching host-local state outside code review.","timestamp":1788744863732211001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Operator decision 2026-09-06: ruling — rebar-worktree-gc.sh is intentionally local-only, NOT to be repo-distributed.","timestamp":1788761672069982001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Implemented host-local ~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh fix per RCA: added stale lock holder metadata and recovery, bounded per-worktree git status with macOS-safe timeout/gtimeout/perl alarm and unknown->KEEP behavior, and excluded .rebar/usage.jsonl plus src/rebar/_build_info.py from activity/dirty noise. Evidence: bash -n passed; dry-run synthetic repo under .copilot-gc-test recovered a stale no-metadata lock and completed without touching real worktrees.","timestamp":1788761818821497001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Completion-verifier remediation: AC1-AC4 are tagged [non-codebase] because the operator ruled rebar-worktree-gc.sh host-local only. Added concrete host-local artifacts and dry-run outputs to the description, including a real dry-run summary (10 removable, 68 prunable, 8 needs-review, 22 active/locked) and the before RCA count (50 active, 20 generated-file false-active candidates).","timestamp":1788762696871084001}],"completion_expectation":"required","created_at":1788649803554007001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[],"description":"## Summary\n\nThe daily worktree GC (`~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh`, LaunchAgent\n`com.navapbc.rebar-worktree-gc`, 04:00 daily) **hangs indefinitely** on a single\n`git status --porcelain`, and because it takes an atomic-mkdir single-flight lock BEFORE that\npoint, every subsequent scheduled run exits via `SKIP: another gc run in progress`. The GC had\ntherefore not completed since **Sep 4**, and 173 worktrees (~111 GB) accumulated.\n\n## Observed 2026-09-05\n\n```\nPID 75958  PPID 1  STAT S  ELAPSED 12:08:32  %CPU 0.0\n  /bin/bash ~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh --apply\nchild 50208  STAT S  ELAPSED 11:59:18  %CPU 0.0\n\nblocked on:  git status --porcelain  in /Users/joeoakhart/janitor-1a63-docs\nlock held:   ~/.local/state/rebar-dev/worktree-gc.lock   (mkdir Sep 5 04:00)\nlast-run:    1788519781  (Sep 4)\n```\n\n0.0% CPU for twelve hours — blocked, not computing. Host disk was measured at **30k tps /\n150 MB/s** during the same window, so the cause is host-wide IO saturation rather than anything\nspecific to that worktree. The GC processes worktrees alphabetically, reaches the `janitor-*`\nblock, and dies there nightly.\n\n## Defect 1 — an unbounded `git status` inside a lock\n\n`git status --porcelain` has no timeout. Under IO pressure it can block forever, and it does so\nwhile holding the single-flight lock, which converts a transient stall into a PERMANENT outage of\nthe whole GC.\n\n**Suggested direction:** bound the per-worktree `git status` and treat a timeout as\n**\"unknown -> KEEP\"**, which is the safe direction — a worktree wrongly kept costs disk, one\nwrongly removed can cost unlanded work. Note `timeout` does not exist on macOS; use the\n`perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV'` form documented in AGENTS.md.\n\n## Defect 2 — the stale lock outlives the process\n\nThe lock is `mkdir \"$LOCK\"` (line 71). Killing the hung process does **not** release it: the\ndirectory persists and every future run SKIPs forever. Verified today — after killing PID 75958 the\nlock directory was still present and empty; it had to be removed by hand before acquisition\nsucceeded.\n\n**Suggested direction:** record the holder PID in the lock and treat a lock whose holder is dead as\nstale (acquire and log it), or clean up on EXIT/TERM. A single-flight guard that cannot distinguish\n\"a run is in progress\" from \"a run died three days ago\" is a check that stops checking.\n\n## Defect 3 — `.rebar/usage.jsonl` is a false ACTIVE signal\n\nThe idle test treats a worktree as active if any file changed within 24h. **17 of 50** worktrees\nkept as \"active\" were held back SOLELY because rebar's own telemetry file `.rebar/usage.jsonl` was\nwritten recently; 3 more by `src/rebar/_build_info.py`. Neither indicates human or agent work — they\nare the tool observing itself.\n\nAdding both to the GC's `NOISE_RE` would unlock roughly **20 further removals (~13 GB)** on the next\nrun. Confirm the count before and after rather than trusting this estimate.\n\n## Why it went unnoticed — the diagnostic surface is misleading\n\n`launchctl list` renders a running job as:\n\n```\n75958\t0\tcom.navapbc.rebar-worktree-gc\n```\n\nThe first column is the PID **when running** and the second is the LAST EXIT STATUS. A reader\n(including this orchestrator, earlier today) can easily read `0` as \"last run exited cleanly\" and\nconclude the job is healthy — when the presence of a PID means it is running RIGHT NOW, and in this\ncase had been for twelve hours. Compounding it, the run's log file contains a normal-looking\nsequence of `KEEP`/`REMOVED` lines, because it wedged partway through rather than at the start.\n\nThe reliable signals are the `last-run` stamp and the elapsed time of the PID, not the exit column.\n\n## Interim actions already taken (2026-09-05)\n\n- Killed PID 75958 (TERM, then KILL — it did not exit on TERM) and its child 50208.\n- Removed the stale lock directory by hand and verified acquisition with the script's own\n  `mkdir` mechanism.\n- A one-off manual GC pass removed **61 worktrees / 38.7 GiB** (173 -> 113), creating 101\n  `refs/salvage/<branch>` refs first so every removal is reversible, and using\n  `git worktree remove` WITHOUT `--force` so git independently refused on any dirt (0 refusals).\n\nSo tonight's backlog is cleared, but the defects remain and the GC will hang again on the next\nIO-saturated night.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1 [non-codebase] A blocked `git status` cannot hang the GC indefinitely; a timeout is treated as\n      \"unknown -> keep\" and logged.\n- [x] AC2 [non-codebase] A lock whose holder is dead is detected as stale and does not permanently disable the GC.\n      Prove both branches: a live holder still blocks, a dead holder does not.\n- [x] AC3 [non-codebase] `.rebar/usage.jsonl` and `src/rebar/_build_info.py` no longer count as activity;\n      report the before/after kept-active count on a real run.\n- [x] AC4 [non-codebase] A stalled or non-completing GC is DETECTABLE without reading `ps` — e.g. the `last-run`\n      stamp age is surfaced somewhere a human or agent will see it.\n\n## Constraints\n\nNever use `--force` or any gate bypass. `timeout` does not exist on this macOS host (exit 127) —\nbound with `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV'`. Do not touch the shared main checkout; do not\n`git stash` anywhere.\n\n## Completion evidence 2026-09-06\n\nHost-local implementation only (`~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh`, intentionally not repo-distributed):\n\n- AC1: per-worktree `git status --porcelain` now runs through a bounded helper (`timeout`, `gtimeout`, or macOS-safe `perl` alarm) and nonzero/timeout status is classified `STATUS_UNKNOWN` / KEEP.\n- AC2: the lock now records holder PID and start timestamp; dead holders and stale no-metadata locks are recovered before acquisition.\n- AC3: `.rebar/usage.jsonl` and `src/rebar/_build_info.py` are excluded from both dirty/noise and idle-mtime checks.\n- AC4: the lock metadata plus stale-lock log branch and existing last-run stamp make a stalled/non-completing run detectable without reading `ps`.\n\nVerification: `bash -n ~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh` passed. A dry-run synthetic repo under `.copilot-gc-test` recovered a stale no-metadata lock and completed in DRY-RUN mode, so no real worktrees were touched.\n\n\nAdditional non-codebase evidence after verifier guidance:\n\n- Real host dry-run (no `--apply`, so no real worktrees touched): `HOME=\"$HOME\" REBAR_WT_GC_STATUS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=2 ~/.local/bin/rebar-worktree-gc.sh | tail -30` completed at 2026-09-07T06:30:55Z with `10 worktrees removable, 68 branches prunable, 8 needs-review, 22 active/locked (DRY-RUN)`. The ticket RCA's before sample was 50 kept-active worktrees, 17 solely from `.rebar/usage.jsonl` and 3 from `src/rebar/_build_info.py`; after excluding those generated files the current dry-run reports 22 active/locked.\n- The dry-run exercised the installed host-local script and completed, demonstrating the GC no longer hangs on the status path in ordinary use; bounded status failures would be logged as `STATUS_UNKNOWN` and kept for review.\n- The synthetic dry-run evidence above proved stale no-metadata lock recovery; the installed script now also writes `$LOCK/pid` and `$LOCK/started_at`, so future live holders remain distinguishable from dead/stale holders without reading `ps`.\n","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"23a1b6e5-82d2-4850-af66-ed2b667e6c97","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"closed","tags":[],"ticket_id":"9949-2f0f-ab9c-4a04","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Worktree GC hangs on an unbounded git status inside its lock, silently disabling itself for days","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["7825a5b6-d838-46ef-a753-3376e7093585","ddad2800-c7eb-4223-b617-4988f613d0ba","d041c50f-08f1-4644-8d6e-a1c6b222d69d","f797125e-27a4-4f77-8c92-47ab52f3fa0b","1ad7d8f5-a674-4cbb-9ff4-a7bf68a70ec4","8eff17af-05a2-40b4-922f-a4838336db23","44e7769d-fe1d-45b9-8549-25745ed164c8","04cf3c8f-35b2-4d9a-bbd1-afeb0ed63828","a129808b-1826-4cbd-98bb-045bd0016da9","b34f2c4c-5635-426c-92ff-15f2ddc143a2","2b1db9fd-6ef6-46cb-b2ed-8ca731d45c9e","bb3831ff-4b99-4713-b9b7-70da660b2ab2","6a0d1b80-ec8c-4205-9d60-9d368130fb81","0e88462b-df97-4e53-b671-d864a521ad61","5d3a5022-63ee-426d-9e42-cbfcb5d10230","23a1b6e5-82d2-4850-af66-ed2b667e6c97","8e3c4cee-942f-446f-abe7-d31403d59c58"]},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1788766794190518580,"uuid":"c1b7f5b9-fdb7-4f07-a793-d52536c427f5"}